      "RWR",
      "[Expert R1] (Detect atomicity violations in the code.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [17, 9, 19]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [17, 11, 19]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[System] Expert first round completed in 4.34 seconds\n\n[Judge R1] (Validate the expert's defect report and check control/interrupt conditions.)\nLet me analyze each read/write operation for the global variable `g`.\n\n## Step 1: Check Control Conditions\n\n### Operations in svp_simple_620_001_isr_1:\n- **Line 9: `g = 5;`** - This is a write operation. There are no control conditions guarding this operation; it will execute whenever the ISR is triggered.\n- **Line 11: `g = 0;`** - This is another write operation. Similarly, there are no control conditions guarding this operation; it will execute whenever the ISR is triggered.\n\n### Operations in svp_simple_620_001_main:\n- **Line 17: `__VERIFIER_assert(g == 0);`** - This contains a read operation of `g` in the assertion. The assertion itself is a control condition, but the read of `g` happens regardless of the assertion's outcome.\n- **Line 19: `__VERIFIER_assert(g == 0);`** - This contains another read operation of `g` in the assertion.\n\n## Step 2: Check Interrupt Status\n\nLooking at the code, there are no `disable_isr()` or `enable_isr()` calls in either the main function or the ISR. This means:\n- All interrupts are enabled by default\n- The ISR `svp_simple_620_001_isr_1` can preempt the main function at any point\n\n## Step 3: Execution Analysis\n\nSince interrupts are always enabled, the ISR can execute at any time during the main function's execution. This creates the following possible interleavings:\n\n1. **Between lines 17 and 19**: The ISR could execute between the two assertions, potentially changing `g` from 0 to 5 and back to 0.\n\n2. **During line 17 or 19**: The ISR could preempt during the evaluation of the assertion, potentially changing `g` mid-evaluation.\n\n## Step 4: Summary of Analysis\n\nAll read/write operations can be executed:\n- The writes in the ISR (lines 9 and 11) can execute whenever the ISR is triggered\n- The reads in the main function (lines 17 and 19) can execute as part of the assertion evaluations\n- Since interrupts are always enabled, the ISR can preempt the main function at any point\n\nThe atomicity violations reported are valid because:\n- The ISR can execute between the two reads in the main function\n- The writes in the ISR can occur between the reads, potentially causing the assertions to fail\n\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [17, 9, 19]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [17, 11, 19]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n[Expert R2] (Refine the report based on judge feedback, re-check for missed or false defects.)\n```json\n{\n    \"violations\": [\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [17, 9, 19]\n        },\n        {\n            \"sharedVariable\": \"g\",\n            \"accessPattern\": [\"Read\", \"Write\", \"Read\"],\n            \"lines\": [17, 11, 19]\n        }\n    ]\n}\n```\n\n"
